  <page>

    <exercise>
      <title>Practice with differential equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3.15. Note: It looks scary, but follow the instructions:
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Apply the <q>shell theorem</q> mentioned in 3.3.1 and Note 3.1. You
                                    <alert>do not</alert> need to prove it, just use the fact that the force
                                    at distance <m>r</m> only comes from the mass enclosed by a sphere of radius
                                    <m>r</m>, which acts like a point mass at its center.      
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Having an expression for the net force on the particle, use Newton's 2nd
                                    law to write the differential equation for <m>\ddot{r}</m>, and by comparing
                                    with the example from class, identify <m>~{\omega=\dfrac{2\pi}{T}}</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Grab a calculator (or use Google/Wolfram) to combine the quantities (with units!)
                                    and find the period.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ul> 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3.25 Hint: the track must have some shape <m>z(r)</m>, use your calculus knowledge + Newton's laws to solve for
                        <m>\dfrac{dz}{dr}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

